WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:03.460
Let's get some more practice
doing implicit differentiation.

00:03.460 --> 00:06.240
So let's find the derivative
of y with respect to x.

00:06.240 --> 00:08.790
We're going to assume
that y is a function of x.

00:08.790 --> 00:11.480
So let's apply our
derivative operator

00:11.480 --> 00:14.560
to both sides of this equation.

00:14.560 --> 00:17.230
So let's apply our
derivative operator.

00:17.230 --> 00:18.820
And so first, on
the left hand side,

00:18.820 --> 00:22.290
we essentially are just going
to apply the chain rule.

00:22.290 --> 00:25.800
First we have the
derivative with respect

00:25.800 --> 00:28.684
to x of x minus y squared.

00:28.684 --> 00:30.100
So the chain rule
tells us this is

00:30.100 --> 00:32.299
going to be the derivative
of the something squared

00:32.299 --> 00:34.300
with respect to the
something, which is just

00:34.300 --> 00:39.090
going to be 2 times x
minus y to the first power.

00:39.090 --> 00:41.610
I won't write the
1 right over there.

00:41.610 --> 00:46.380
Times the derivative of the
something with respect to x.

00:46.380 --> 00:50.420
Well, the derivative of x
with respect to x is just 1,

00:50.420 --> 00:52.124
and the derivative
of y with respect

00:52.124 --> 00:53.790
to x, that's what
we're trying to solve.

00:53.790 --> 00:58.540
So it's going to
be 1 minus dy dx.

00:58.540 --> 01:00.157
Let me make it a
little bit clearer

01:00.157 --> 01:01.490
what I just did right over here.

01:01.490 --> 01:04.410
This right over here
is the derivative

01:04.410 --> 01:12.000
of x minus y squared with
respect to x minus y.

01:15.210 --> 01:18.770
And then this right over
here is the derivative

01:18.770 --> 01:23.710
of x minus y with respect to x.

01:23.710 --> 01:25.610
Just the chain rule.

01:25.610 --> 01:27.930
Now let's go to the right
hand side of this equation.

01:27.930 --> 01:31.510
This is going to be equal to
the derivative of x with respect

01:31.510 --> 01:32.760
to x is 1.

01:32.760 --> 01:34.810
The derivative of y
with respect to x.

01:34.810 --> 01:37.350
We're just going to write
that as the derivative of y

01:37.350 --> 01:38.565
with respect to x.

01:38.565 --> 01:40.440
And then finally, the
derivative with respect

01:40.440 --> 01:43.680
to x of a constant, that's
just going to be equal to 0.

01:43.680 --> 01:49.630
Now let's see if we can
solve for the derivative of y

01:49.630 --> 01:51.610
with respect to x.

01:51.610 --> 01:53.477
So the most obvious thing to do.

01:53.477 --> 01:54.310
Let's make it clear.

01:54.310 --> 01:57.170
This right over here, I
can rewrite as 2x minus 2y.

01:57.170 --> 01:59.570
So let me do that so
I can save some space.

01:59.570 --> 02:04.050
This is 2x minus 2y If
I just distribute the 2.

02:04.050 --> 02:06.920
And now I can distribute
the 2x minus 2y

02:06.920 --> 02:10.240
onto each of these terms.

02:10.240 --> 02:16.840
So 2x minus 2y times 1 is
just going to be 2x minus 2y.

02:16.840 --> 02:21.180
And then 2x minus 2y
times negative dy dx,

02:21.180 --> 02:24.320
that's just going to be
negative 2x minus 2y.

02:24.320 --> 02:29.860
Or we could write that as
2y minus 2x times dy dx.

02:34.430 --> 02:37.320
Is equal to 1 plus dy dx.

02:39.910 --> 02:41.940
I'll do all my dy
dx's in orange now.

02:41.940 --> 02:46.480
1 plus dy dx.

02:46.480 --> 02:48.230
So now there's a
couple of things

02:48.230 --> 02:49.950
that we could attempt to do.

02:49.950 --> 02:55.350
We could subtract 2x
minus 2y from both sides.

02:55.350 --> 02:56.640
So let's do that.

02:56.640 --> 03:01.510
So let's subtract 2x
minus 2y from both sides.

03:01.510 --> 03:06.230
So over here, we're going
to subtract 2x minus

03:06.230 --> 03:08.410
2y from that side.

03:08.410 --> 03:11.660
And then we could also subtract
a dy dx from both sides,

03:11.660 --> 03:13.940
so that all of our dy dx's
are on the left hand side,

03:13.940 --> 03:16.680
and all of our non dy dx's
are on the right hand side.

03:16.680 --> 03:18.090
So let's do that.

03:18.090 --> 03:26.860
So we're going to subtract a
dy dx on the right and a dy dx

03:26.860 --> 03:28.030
here on the left.

03:28.030 --> 03:29.380
And so what are we left with?

03:32.620 --> 03:35.890
Well, on the left hand
side, these cancel out.

03:35.890 --> 03:40.940
And we're left with 2y minus
2x dy dx minus 1 dy dx,

03:40.940 --> 03:42.825
or just minus a dy dx.

03:42.825 --> 03:43.700
Let me make it clear.

03:43.700 --> 03:48.540
We could write this
as a minus 1 dy dx.

03:48.540 --> 03:50.650
So this is we can
essentially just add

03:50.650 --> 03:52.110
these two coefficients.

03:52.110 --> 03:58.540
So this simplifies to 2y minus
2x minus 1 times the derivative

03:58.540 --> 04:03.510
of y with respect
to x, which is going

04:03.510 --> 04:07.850
to be equal to-- on this
side, this cancels out.

04:07.850 --> 04:12.104
We are left with 1
minus 2x plus 2y.

04:12.104 --> 04:13.270
So let me write it that way.

04:13.270 --> 04:17.529
Or we could write
this as-- so negative,

04:17.529 --> 04:20.839
negative 2y is
just a positive 2y.

04:20.839 --> 04:23.150
And then we have minus 2x.

04:23.150 --> 04:26.030
And then we add that 1, plus 1.

04:26.030 --> 04:27.990
And now to solve
for dy dx, we just

04:27.990 --> 04:31.760
have to divide both sides
by 2y minus 2x minus 1.

04:31.760 --> 04:33.200
And we are left
with-- we deserve

04:33.200 --> 04:35.190
a little bit of a drum
roll at this point.

04:35.190 --> 04:36.981
As you can see, the
hardest part was really

04:36.981 --> 04:39.170
the algebra to solve for dy dx.

04:39.170 --> 04:41.550
We get the derivative
of y with respect

04:41.550 --> 04:48.870
to x is equal to
2y minus 2x plus 1

04:48.870 --> 04:55.210
over 2y minus 2x minus 1.
